{{also|Appendix:Variations of "mis"}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|enm|en}} {{m|enm|mis-}}, from {{etyl|ang|en}} {{m|ang|mis-||mis-}}, from Proto-Germanic {{m|gem-pro|*missa-||wrongly, badly}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|en}} {{m|ine-pro|*mitto||mutual, reciprocal}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|en}} {{m|ine-pro|*meyt-||to replace, switch, exchange, swap}}. Cognate with {{cog|sco|mis-||mis-}}, {{cog|nl|mis-||mis-}}, {{cog|de|miss-}}, {{m|de|mis-||mis-}}, {{cog|sv|mis-||mis-}}, {{cog|is|mis-||mis-}}. Compare also {{cog|fr|més-}}, {{m|fr|mé-||mis-}}, from {{etyl|fro|en}} {{m|fro|mes-||mis-}}, from {{etyl|frk|en}} {{m|frk|*mis-}}, {{m|frk|*missa-||mis-}}, from the same Proto-Germanic source above.

===Prefix===
{{en-prefix}}

# [[bad]], [[badly]], [[wrong]], [[wrongly]]
# [[lack]] or [[failure]]
# [[in error]]
#: ''I '''mis'''deleted my file yesterday and had to have it restored.''

====Derived terms====
{{prefixsee|en}}

====See also====
* {{l|en|mal-}}
* {{l|en|miso-}}

